#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:9
msgid "Understanding nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:11
msgid ""
"**Goal:** Learn about the function of nodes in ROS 2, and the tools to "
"interact with them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:13
msgid "**Tutorial level:** Beginner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:15
msgid "**Time:** 10 minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:19
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:22
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:25
msgid "1 The ROS 2 graph"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:27
msgid ""
"Over the next few tutorials, you will learn about a series of core ROS 2 "
"concepts that make up what is referred to as the \"ROS (2) graph\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:29
msgid ""
"The ROS graph is a network of ROS 2 elements processing data together at the "
"same time. It encompasses all executables and the connections between them "
"if you were to map them all out and visualize them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:33
msgid "2 Nodes in ROS 2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:35
msgid ""
"Each node in ROS should be responsible for a single, modular purpose, e.g. "
"controlling the wheel motors or publishing the sensor data from a laser "
"range-finder. Each node can send and receive data from other nodes via "
"topics, services, actions, or parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:40
msgid ""
"A full robotic system is comprised of many nodes working in concert. In ROS "
"2, a single executable (C++ program, Python program, etc.) can contain one "
"or more nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:44
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:46
msgid ""
"The :doc:`previous tutorial <../../../../Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/"
"Introducing-Turtlesim>` shows you how to install the ``turtlesim`` package "
"used here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:48
msgid ""
"As always, don't forget to source ROS 2 in :doc:`every new terminal you open "
"<../../../../Get-Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:51
msgid "Tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:54
msgid "1 ros2 run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:56
msgid "The command ``ros2 run`` launches an executable from a package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:58
msgid "$ ros2 run <package_name> <executable_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:62
msgid "To run turtlesim, open a new terminal, and enter the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:64
msgid "$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:68
msgid ""
"The turtlesim window will open, as you saw in the :doc:`previous tutorial "
"<../../../../Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:70
msgid ""
"Here, the package name is ``turtlesim`` and the executable name is "
"``turtlesim_node``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:72
msgid ""
"We still don't know the node name, however. You can find node names by using "
"``ros2 node list``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:76
msgid "2 ros2 node list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:78
msgid ""
"``ros2 node list`` will show you the names of all running nodes. This is "
"especially useful when you want to interact with a node, or when you have a "
"system running many nodes and need to keep track of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:81
msgid ""
"Open a new terminal while turtlesim is still running in the other one, and "
"enter the following command. The terminal will return the node name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:84
msgid ""
"$ ros2 node list\n"
"/turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:89
msgid "Open another new terminal and start the teleop node with the command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:91
msgid "$ ros2 run turtlesim turtle_teleop_key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:95
msgid ""
"Here, we are referring to the ``turtlesim`` package again, but this time we "
"target the executable named ``turtle_teleop_key``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:97
msgid ""
"Return to the terminal where you ran ``ros2 node list`` and run it again. "
"You will now see the names of two active nodes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:100
msgid ""
"$ ros2 node list\n"
"/turtlesim\n"
"/teleop_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:107
msgid "2.1 Remapping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:109
msgid ""
"`Remapping <https://design.ros2.org/articles/ros_command_line_arguments."
"html#name-remapping-rules>`__ allows you to reassign default node "
"properties, like node name, topic names, service names, etc., to custom "
"values. In the last tutorial, you used remapping on ``turtle_teleop_key`` to "
"change the cmd_vel topic and target **turtle2**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:112
msgid ""
"Now, let's reassign the name of our ``/turtlesim`` node. In a new terminal, "
"run the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:115
msgid ""
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node --ros-args --remap __node:=my_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:119
msgid ""
"Since you're calling ``ros2 run`` on turtlesim again, another turtlesim "
"window will open. However, now if you return to the terminal where you ran "
"``ros2 node list``, and run it again, you will see three node names:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:122
msgid ""
"/my_turtle\n"
"/turtlesim\n"
"/teleop_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:129
msgid "3 ros2 node info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:131
msgid ""
"Now that you know the names of your nodes, you can access more information "
"about them with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:133
msgid "$ ros2 node info <node_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:137
msgid "To examine your latest node, ``my_turtle``, run the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:139
msgid ""
"$ ros2 node info /my_turtle\n"
"/my_turtle\n"
"  Subscribers:\n"
"    /parameter_events: rcl_interfaces/msg/ParameterEvent\n"
"    /turtle1/cmd_vel: geometry_msgs/msg/Twist\n"
"  Publishers:\n"
"    /parameter_events: rcl_interfaces/msg/ParameterEvent\n"
"    /rosout: rcl_interfaces/msg/Log\n"
"    /turtle1/color_sensor: turtlesim_msgs/msg/Color\n"
"    /turtle1/pose: turtlesim_msgs/msg/Pose\n"
"  Service Servers:\n"
"    /clear: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"    /kill: turtlesim_msgs/srv/Kill\n"
"    /my_turtle/describe_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/DescribeParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/get_parameter_types: rcl_interfaces/srv/GetParameterTypes\n"
"    /my_turtle/get_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/GetParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/list_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/ListParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/set_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/SetParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/set_parameters_atomically: rcl_interfaces/srv/"
"SetParametersAtomically\n"
"    /reset: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"    /spawn: turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn\n"
"    /turtle1/set_pen: turtlesim_msgs/srv/SetPen\n"
"    /turtle1/teleport_absolute: turtlesim_msgs/srv/TeleportAbsolute\n"
"    /turtle1/teleport_relative: turtlesim_msgs/srv/TeleportRelative\n"
"  Service Clients:\n"
"\n"
"  Action Servers:\n"
"    /turtle1/rotate_absolute: turtlesim_msgs/action/RotateAbsolute\n"
"  Action Clients:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:171
msgid ""
"``ros2 node info`` returns a list of subscribers, publishers, services, and "
"actions. i.e. the ROS graph connections that interact with that node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:174
msgid ""
"Now try running the same command on the ``/teleop_turtle`` node, and see how "
"its connections differ from ``my_turtle``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:176
msgid ""
"You will learn more about the ROS graph connection concepts including the "
"message types in the upcoming tutorials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:179
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:181
msgid ""
"A node is a fundamental ROS 2 element that serves a single, modular purpose "
"in a robotics system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:183
msgid ""
"In this tutorial, you utilized nodes created in the ``turtlesim`` package by "
"running the executables ``turtlesim_node`` and ``turtle_teleop_key``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:185
msgid ""
"You learned how to use ``ros2 node list`` to discover active node names and "
"``ros2 node info`` to introspect a single node. These tools are vital to "
"understanding the flow of data in a complex, real-world robot system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:189
msgid "Next steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:191
msgid ""
"Now that you understand nodes in ROS 2, you can move on to the :doc:`topics "
"tutorial <../../../interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-"
"ROS2-Topics>`. Topics are one of the communication types that connects nodes."
msgstr ""

